  <bf:Work rdf:about="http://id.loc.gov/resources/works/in00024462878">
    <bflc:aap>Objects and the grammar of countability</bflc:aap>
    <bflc:aap-normalized>objectsandthegrammarofcountability</bflc:aap-normalized>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://id.loc.gov/ontologies/bibframe/Text"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://id.loc.gov/ontologies/bibframe/Monograph"/>
    <bf:language>
      <bf:Language rdf:about="http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/languages/eng">
	<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">English</rdfs:label>
	<bf:code rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">eng</bf:code>
      </bf:Language>
    </bf:language>
    <bf:title>
      <bf:Title>
	<bf:mainTitle>Objects and the grammar of countability</bf:mainTitle>
      </bf:Title>
    </bf:title>
    <bf:content>
      <bf:Content rdf:about="http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/contentTypes/txt">
	<rdfs:label>text</rdfs:label>
	<bf:code>txt</bf:code>
      </bf:Content>
    </bf:content>
    <bf:summary>
      <bf:Summary>
	<rdfs:label>"This book proposes an object-centred and contextualist account of the count/mass distinction. Our main empirical finding is that there is a connection between variation in count/mass lexicalization patterns across and within languages and the ways in which nouns, mass or count, can diverge from manifesting the canonical grammatical reflexes expected of the count/mass distinction in a given language. No extant count/mass theory can motivate this striking connection. We propose that mass-count pairs of nouns (across and within languages) lexicalize the same number-neutral core property that is satisfied by objects, defined both in mereotopological and affordance-based terms. Context plays a crucial role, since it influences different ways of viewing the entities in the extension of a noun and so what counts as 'one' for that noun in that context. We propose a system of three perceptual-interactive constraints, based upon how we perceive and interact with objects, that predict the propensity for a core property that is satisfied by objects to nonetheless be lexicalized as mass. These constraints are also key to explaining variation in the count/mass lexicalization patterns of such properties. We show how the extent to which a core property can satisfy these constraints can be estimated from a corpus, and how these corpus measures can be used to model variation in count/mass lexicalization patterns. The main data relies on concrete common nouns in number-marking languages, namely Czech, English, Finnish, and German, but we also extend our account to abstract nouns, and to two non-number-marking languages, Mandarin and Yudja"-- Provided by publisher.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:Summary>
    </bf:summary>
